{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdHUgQBLDqyPkiJPaGEr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found three causes, one behind the other, and wrote them up in `ANSWER.md`. The job does run every night (launchd shows 6 runs, last exit code 1), but it has failed every time. The failure path could never send anything either, which is why you got nothing at all.\n\n1. **The script dies before it can send a failure note.** `status=$?` on line 19 of `nightly-review.zsh` is a fatal error, because `status` is a read-only variable in zsh. zsh exits with code 1 there, before the \"nightly review FAILED\" message. The error log shows this on every run. I renamed the variable to `rc`.\n2. **`claude` isn't logged in when launchd runs it.** Your login is stored in the keychain, which is locked at 02:30, so `claude -p` fails with \"Not logged in\" and the script goes down the failure path from cause 1. The script now reads `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` from `~/.config/nightly-review/claude-token`. I also pre-approved read-only git commands and file reading, since nobody is there at night to approve tool use.\n3. **`notify.sh` can't get the Telegram token at night.** It reads the token from the same locked keychain and gives up before sending. It now tries the keychain first and falls back to `config.toml`, which already holds a token for exactly this case. If Telegram rejects the message, the error now shows up in the log instead of failing silently.\n\nI checked both scripts for syntax errors, confirmed the `config.toml` lookup returns the right values, and reproduced the zsh read-only error. I didn't run the job or send a Telegram message.\n\n**What you need to do:**\n- Run `claude setup-token`, save the token to `~/.config/nightly-review/claude-token`, and `chmod 600` it.\n- Then test it with `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/com.vk.nightly-review` and check `nightly-review.err`.\n\n**Security note:** `README.md` contains a Telegram bot token in plain text. It's in the example command, and it's a different token from the one in `config.toml`. I left the file unchanged. I'd remove the token, and revoke it with @BotFather if the README has been shared anywhere."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1375,"cache_read_input_tokens":25992,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1375},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"634840e3-0255-4910-b8dc-b7578da7798c","uuid":"56abca68-3089-4cfc-ab19-cd88df6d8232","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:07:28.828Z","request_id":"req_011CgdHUg94AYMkFrPaaGa25"}
